Cell["\<\
It is always a good idea to plot the individual points in the impulse \
response function to check that the sampling rate is high enough given the \
time course of the function. It is best to have plenty of points (e.g., > 5) \
covering the initial rise of the impulse response function. It is also best \
to have a domain (whose last value is \[Tau]Max) which is 1.5 x to 2 x the \
time it takes for the impulse response to \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]die out\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. If these conditions are not met then resample the \
original continuous (symbolic) impulse response function with a more \
appropriate sampling interval, \[CapitalDelta]\[Tau], and maximum lag, \
\[Tau]Max.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.783881722767273*^9, 3.78388183095761*^9}, {
  3.783948142415991*^9, 
  3.78394835713749*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ec239ab0-3fac-4299-91de-\
8f1136ec0a3a"],

Cell["\<\
Note that the area under the impulse response function should equal the \
static gain, which is 1.0 in this case. Lets check by crude rectangular \
numeric integration:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.783716830577589*^9, 3.78371685279784*^9}, {
  3.783948367532976*^9, 3.7839484440018005`*^9}, {3.783948533686204*^9, 
  3.783948536844319*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a7c9a5fc-565e-4d69-9d84-\
d30a253f2a0e"],

